  "marks": "Mastering: A sustained, coherent staging concept across all disciplines, anchored to the themes of conscience and reputation. Set is described specifically (e.g. a bare stone-flagged floor, a single table, two chairs — the prison stripped of everything except the paper on the table, which becomes the visual focus of the scene; perhaps a small high window letting in cold dawn light); lighting is specific and named (e.g. opening with a dim, cold Fresnel wash that flattens the cell; a gradual narrowing during the signing sequence until a single tight profile special falls on Proctor's hands as he signs; a held pause at the tear; then a slow fade to black after Elizabeth's final line, the stage light leaving as if the world itself is extinguishing); sound is named and purposeful (e.g. near-total silence; the faint sound of the congregation praying outside as a diegetic intrusion — the community Proctor is dying for — then silence at the tear, then a single tolling bell as the blackout completes); performer choices are precise (Proctor's slow, controlled movement throughout; absolute stillness from Hale and Danforth; Elizabeth not moving but holding eye contact with Proctor; the tear slow and quiet; the body held upright after it as a final physical statement); every choice is justified with reference to the themes. Substantiated directorial judgement.\n\nSecure: All disciplines addressed with mostly concrete choices; both themes engaged; generally accurate effect on audience; some named technical detail.\n\nDeveloping: Generic choices across most disciplines ('dim lighting', 'quiet music'); limited named technical detail; themes named but not developed in relation to staging choices.\n\nEmerging: Basic list of ideas; one or more disciplines absent; themes mentioned but staging choices not connected to them."
