 {
  "text": "How does Miller use the parallel between Salem 1692 and McCarthyite America to communicate his intentions about the relationship between institutional power and individual conscience? Refer to specific aspects of the play's context.",
  "type": "12 marks — Analyse Intentions",
  "marks": "Mastering: The response analyses the allegorical structure precisely: Salem 1692 is the literal story; HUAC 1950-54 is the intended parallel. The candidate identifies specific structural parallels (accusation without reliable evidence; the demand to name names; blacklisting as social death vs hanging as literal death; the rewarding of confession and naming over refusal and silence). Miller's personal position is noted (his own HUAC subpoena in 1956, his refusal to name names, his identification with Proctor). The response analyses what Miller wants the audience to understand — not just that McCarthyism was unjust, but that the same machinery of fear can operate in any society that prioritises institutional self-preservation over individual due process. Staging implications are noted (e.g. how abstract staging choices let the allegory breathe across time). Judgement is substantiated and shows understanding of the play as a political act.\n\nSecure: Identifies the Salem-McCarthy parallel accurately; notes Miller's own position; analyses at least two specific structural parallels; some reference to audience effect; limited staging reference.\n\nDeveloping: Describes the historical context without analysing Miller's allegorical purpose; limited connection between the two timescales; audience effect asserted rather than developed.\n\nEmerging: Basic description of one historical context with minimal analytical engagement; allegory not addressed."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are a director making a production of The Crucible for a contemporary audience. Discuss how you would use performer-audience configuration, set and costume to communicate the play's allegorical purpose — that the pattern of unjust accusation belongs to every age, not only to 1692 Salem.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering: The response offers a coherent directorial concept that prioritises the allegorical over the historically specific. Performer-audience configuration is argued and justified (e.g. in the round — the audience is surrounded by and implicated in the community; or a thrust stage that pushes the court scene into the audience's space, making them uncomfortable observers; the choice is explicitly argued, not merely stated); set design is specific and abstract (e.g. a bare raised platform for the court that could be any century's dock; a single wooden table for the domestic scenes; the set stripped of period ornament so that its austerity resonates universally; perhaps a back wall that begins as rough stone and is progressively lit to suggest different institutional spaces — courtroom, committee room, prison); costume is specific and argued (e.g. court officials in severe monochrome that deliberately avoids period specificity — the black is contemporary bureaucracy as much as Puritan severity; the accused in undyed, rough natural fabrics that give them warmth and individuality against the officials' uniformity; Tituba's costume in warmer earth tones that mark her as doubly marginalised — the first accused because she is the easiest to sacrifice); every choice is justified with reference to the allegorical purpose; substantiated directorial judgement about what the audience should understand and feel as they leave the theatre.\n\nSecure: All three disciplines addressed with mostly concrete choices; allegorical purpose engaged; performer-audience configuration chosen and defended; generally accurate effect on audience.\n\nDeveloping: Ideas present but generic; limited named technical detail; allegorical purpose mentioned but staging choices not fully connected to it.\n\nEmerging: Basic list of ideas; one or more disciplines absent; historical context described but allegorical staging not addressed."
 }
