  "marks": "Mastering: Selects and justifies a staging configuration (e.g. thrust, so the audience feel themselves surrounded by the courtroom — positioned as jurors rather than spectators). Describes at least two named lighting choices with specific elements (e.g. cold white profile lanterns steeply angled to create downward shadows across all faces; a tight special on Danforth from above using a profile spot without gel, emphasising his institutional authority; a gobo projecting a grid or lattice pattern onto the stage floor suggesting a cage or a scaffold). Explains how each design choice communicates the Brechtian intention (the audience cannot get lost in individual sympathy — the staging keeps reminding them of the political structure). Links to the allegory explicitly.\n\nSecure: Names at least two design choices in at least two disciplines (configuration + lighting) with generally concrete elements. Effect on audience explained in terms of the Brechtian approach. Generally specific.\n\nDeveloping: Refers to 'Brechtian staging' and 'harsh lighting' without naming specific elements. Configuration mentioned but not justified. Effect on audience implied.\n\nEmerging: States that the courtroom should look serious or imposing. No named design elements. No connection to Brecht or to Miller's intentions."
